The main difference between HTTP cookies and storage cookies is that HTTP cookies are used to track and personalize a user's session on a website, while storage cookies track and store personal information about the user:

HTTP cookies
Storage cookies
Purpose
HTTP cookies -> Track and personalize a user's session on a website

Storage cookies -> Track and store personal information about the user
Examples of information stored
User preferences and activity
Name, residential address, email address, and phone number

HTTP cookies are created to identify users when they visit a website. They can also be used to track user behavior across multiple sites. 

Storage cookies can last on a user's browser for seconds to years, depending on how long the user has set the cookie to expire. 


